Since you reverted to the original ValidationMasterService, you already have the exact database logic needed for your new API. Your getAllActiveCurrencies() method is already designed to pull strictly the CURRENCY_CODE where FLAG = 1.
To expose this as your own custom API for the frontend (or other services) to consume, you simply need to create a new REST Controller that calls this existing method.
Here is the exact file to create to build your own Currency API.
### 1. Create the Master Data Controller
Create a new controller file. This will expose an endpoint (e.g., /api/v1/master/currencies) that returns just the list of active 3-letter currency codes as JSON.
**File:** src/main/java/com/fincore/VoucherService/Controllers/MasterDataController.java
```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Controllers;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.ValidationMasterService;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import java.util.Set;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/master")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class MasterDataController {

    private final ValidationMasterService validationMasterService;

    /**
     * API Endpoint: GET /api/v1/master/currencies
     * Purpose: Returns a list of only the active CURRENCY_CODEs.
     */
    @GetMapping("/currencies")
    public ResponseEntity<Set<String>> getActiveCurrencies() {
        // Reuses your existing high-performance JDBC method
        Set<String> currencies = validationMasterService.getAllActiveCurrencies();
        
        return ResponseEntity.ok(currencies);
    }
}

```
### How this works:
 1. When your frontend hits GET /api/v1/master/currencies, this controller catches the request.
 2. It triggers your exact original getAllActiveCurrencies() method.
 3. The method uses the jdbcTemplate with your fetchSize(1000) to pull just the CURRENCY_CODE from Oracle.
 4. It wraps it in a standard HTTP 200 OK response.
The JSON output will look exactly like this, giving the frontend exactly what it needs without exposing the rest of the table's data:
```json
[
  "USD",
  "INR",
  "EUR",
  "GBP"
]

```
You can use this exact same controller pattern to create /api/v1/master/branches or /api/v1/master/cgls later on if you want to expose those specific codes to the frontend as well!
